      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\n                \"Read\",\n                \"Write\",\n                \"Read\"\n            ],\n            \"lines\": [\n                22,\n                12,\n                24\n            ]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\n                \"Read\",\n                \"Write\",\n                \"Read\"\n            ],\n            \"lines\": [\n                22,\n                14,\n                24\n            ]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\n                \"Read\",\n                \"Write\",\n                \"Read\"\n            ],\n            \"lines\": [\n                22,\n                16,\n                24\n            ]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[System] Expert first round completed in 18.72 seconds\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\n### Accessibility Analysis Summary\n\n**1. Read of `g` at line 22 in `svp_simple_631_001_main`:**\n- **Control Conditions:** There are no control statements (`if`, `while`, etc.) guarding this operation.\n- **Execution Analysis:** This operation is located in the `svp_simple_631_001_main` function. It is executed unconditionally when the program starts.\n- **Conclusion:** This operation is **accessible**.\n\n**2. Write of `g` at line 12 in `svp_simple_631_001_isr_1`:**\n- **Control Conditions:** There are no control statements guarding this operation within the ISR.\n- **Execution Analysis:** This operation is inside an ISR. The code does not contain any `disable_isr()` calls. In a preemptive multitasking environment, it is assumed that interrupts are enabled by default. Therefore, `svp_simple_631_001_isr_1` can preempt the `main` function. If the ISR is triggered, this write will be executed.\n- **Conclusion:** This operation is **accessible**.\n\n**3. Write of `g` at line 14 in `svp_simple_631_001_isr_1`:**\n- **Control Conditions:** There are no control statements guarding this operation within the ISR.\n- **Execution Analysis:** This operation is executed sequentially after the write at line 12 within the same ISR. If the ISR is triggered, this write will be executed.\n- **Conclusion:** This operation is **accessible**.\n\n**4. Write of `g` at line 16 in `svp_simple_631_001_isr_1`:**\n- **Control Conditions:** There are no control statements guarding this operation within the ISR.\n- **Execution Analysis:** This operation is executed sequentially after the write at line 14 within the same ISR. If the ISR is triggered, this write will be executed.\n- **Conclusion:** This operation is **accessible**.\n\n**5. Read of `g` at line 24 in `svp_simple_631_001_main`:**\n- **Control Conditions:** There are no control statements guarding this operation.\n- **Execution Analysis:** This operation follows the read at line 22 in the `main` function. It is executed unconditionally.\n- **Conclusion:** This operation is **accessible**.\n\n### Final Decision\n\nAll read and write operations listed in the defect report are accessible. An interrupt can occur between the read at line 22 and the read at line 24 in the `main` function. When the interrupt `svp_simple_631_001_isr_1` is handled, it executes all three writes to the shared variable `g` (lines 12, 14, and 16). This interleaving violates the atomicity of the read-read sequence in `main`. Therefore, all three reported violations are valid.\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\n                \"Read\",\n                \"Write\",\n                \"Read\"\n            ],\n            \"lines\": [\n                22,\n                12,\n                24\n            ]\n